import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="XZ Columnar Terrain | Aaron’s Dev Experiments">
	<article class="article-page">
		<header class="article-header">
			<p class="eyebrow">
				<a href="/areas/terrain-systems">Terrain Systems</a>
			</p>

			<h1>XZ Columnar Terrain</h1>

			<p class="intro">
				An experiment in constructing a sealed voxel-owned terrain
				surface from heightmap-derived planar patches and vertical side
				fragments.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="article-content">
			<h2>The Visual Inspiration</h2>

			<p>
				The original goal of this experiment was to build a genuinely 3D
				voxel world that possessed the visual language of a tiled game.
				Something akin to an old 2D tile-based game, but taken to an
				extreme.
			</p>

			<p>
				A large part of that idea came from tactical role-playing games
				(SRPGs)—games like Disgaea and Final Fantasy Tactics. Games like
				these make the underlying grid extremely easy to read. Movement,
				elevation, and the relationship between neighboring spaces are
				all communicated through discrete tiles.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure">
				<img
					src="/images/spectral-force-3-grid-reference.png"
					alt="A tactical role-playing game battlefield with a continuous 
					terrain surface and a visible movement grid overlaid on top."
				/>

				<figcaption>
					<em>Spectral Force 3</em> uses a clearly readable tactical grid
					over otherwise continuous terrain. Screenshot source:
					{" "}
					<a
						href="https://gamefabrique.com/storage/screenshots/xbox360/spectral-force-3-innocent-rage-03.png"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
					>
						GameFabrique
					</a>.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				While older games tended to convey the grid through the terrain
				itself, many modern examples use continuous terrain and
				emphasize the grid through movement or targeting overlays. What
				interested me was taking that visual language further. I wanted
				the terrain surface itself to retain an obvious tiled structure,
				even when no gameplay overlay was present.
			</p>

			<p>
				The goal was not to hide the grid. I wanted the grid to be part
				of the aesthetic.
			</p>

			<h2>The Goal</h2>

			<p>
				The visual idea only worked if the world remained genuinely
				three-dimensional underneath it.
			</p>

			<p>
				I did not set out to construct a conventional heightmap with a
				tile grid drawn over the terrain. The long-term goal was a
				volumetric world that could eventually support arbitrary
				editing, caves, overhangs, tunnels, and other features that
				cannot be represented by a simple height field.
			</p>

			<p>
				That meant retaining voxels as the underlying spatial structure.
				Individual voxels could be empty, filled, or intersected by the
				terrain surface.
			</p>

			<p>
				The interesting problem was what happened with those surface
				voxels. Rather than exposing cube faces or allowing an arbitrary
				smooth surface to pass through them, I wanted the visible
				terrain to resolve into deliberate, flat, rectangular tiles.
			</p>

			<p>So the central question became:</p>

			<p>
				<em>
					How can a continuous 3D terrain surface be approximated by
					rectangular tiles without giving up the underlying voxel
					structure?
				</em>
			</p>

			<h2>The Split from Ordinary Voxel Terrain</h2>

			<p>
				A conventional block-based voxel world already solves several
				difficult problems very neatly.
			</p>

			<p>
				Each voxel has clear ownership. Editing is straightforward. The
				world naturally forms a sealed volume, collision is relatively
				simple, and the relationship between the underlying data and the
				rendered geometry is easy to understand.
			</p>

			<p>
				The problem was that the visual result was not what I was after.
			</p>

			<p>
				I wanted to keep the useful structural properties of voxels
				without being limited to their exposed faces. The surface needed
				to move through the voxel grid rather than simply sit on top of
				it.
			</p>

			<p>
				At the same time, wherever that surface passed through the
				world, I wanted it to resolve into flat, rectangular regions
				rather than forming an arbitrary smooth surface.
			</p>

			<p>
				That combination is what created the geometry problem that drove
				the rest of the experiment.
			</p>

			<h2>Turning a Continuous Surface into Tiles</h2>

			<p>
				The first problem was reconciling a continuous terrain surface
				with a discrete voxel grid. It is straightforward enough to
				determine which voxels are intersected by the surface, but that
				information alone is insufficient to construct the kind of
				terrain I wanted.
			</p>

			<p>
				Not every voxel touched by the surface is a good candidate for a
				rectangular surface tile. In some cases, the surface passes
				cleanly through much of the voxel. In others, only a tiny sliver
				clips one corner or edge. Treating both cases as equivalent
				“surface voxels” creates an immediate problem: there may not be
				enough meaningful surface inside a given voxel to justify
				replacing it with a full rectangular plane. Compounding this
				issue, if you start picking and choosing which surface voxels
				you want to use and which you want to throw away, you travel
				further and further away from the original continuous
				surface—not to mention from a complete surface at all.
			</p>

			<p>
				I explored that problem separately for a while. One experiment
				attempted to use the actual surface/voxel intersections
				themselves to construct a usable surface representation. The
				idea was appealing because it stayed closely tied to the
				underlying geometry, but the resulting intersection shapes were
				too irregular to satisfy the tiled-surface constraint. The
				problem there can be summed up simply: when a plane intersects a
				cube, the resulting polygon is often not rectangular. So then I
				was right back to the problem of deciding which representations
				to keep and which to throw away.
			</p>

			<p>
				I then realized I needed a way to guarantee that a rectangular
				surface could be generated. It also dawned on me that trying to
				force an individual voxel to create one was probably not the
				best approach, so I began to move away from treating individual
				voxels as the unit from which the visible surface should be
				constructed.
			</p>

			<p>
				Toward that end, a heightmap offered a useful simplification.
				Instead of considering one voxel at a time, I could consider an
				entire vertical XZ column. Because a heightmap is continuous and
				defines a single height at every XZ position, the terrain
				surface is guaranteed to span the entire rectangular footprint
				of the column.
			</p>

			<p>
				That gave me something the individual-voxel approach could not:
				a reliable rectangular domain with meaningful surface
				information at all four corners.
			</p>

			<p>
				All the while, there was one additional constraint that shaped
				every approach I tried. I did not want a tile's surface
				representation to depend on the surface representations
				constructed by its neighbors. Each column needed to be capable
				of determining its own surface independently from the terrain
				information around it. Neighboring tiles should meet because
				they were derived from the same underlying terrain field, not
				because one tile had to inspect or modify another tile's
				geometry. This was also meant to keep the algorithmic complexity
				constrained while serving as a steppingstone toward a more
				generalized version of the technique.
			</p>

			<p>
				Once I had moved to columns, the next question became how to
				reduce the continuous surface inside that rectangular footprint
				to a single plane.
			</p>

			<p>
				My first thought was to take the tangent plane of the original
				continuous surface at the center of the column. That works, but
				it only describes the behavior of the surface at one point. It
				says very little about how the surface behaves across the rest
				of the column.
			</p>

			<p>
				I then tried several ways of using the four corner intersections
				directly to construct a plane. The difficulty is that four
				points sampled from a curved surface generally do not lie on the
				same plane. Any single plane chosen from them necessarily throws
				away some of the information contained in the original surface,
				and the approaches I tried never really gave a great
				approximation.
			</p>

			<p>
				That eventually led to a different idea: instead of trying to
				fit a plane directly to the raw samples, first construct a
				smooth local surface over the column, and then derive the planar
				tile from that approximation.
			</p>

			<p>That is where the bicubic patch entered the experiment.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="article-project">
